PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator relies on mutually exclusive RNA secondary structures in the trpL leader transcript. Regions 3 and 4 base-pair to create the rho-independent terminator hairpin (followed by the run of U residues) that aborts transcription when tryptophan is high. Under these conditions the ribosome does not stall at the tandem Trp codons in region 1, so it covers region 2 and thereby prevents formation of the competing 2–3 antiterminator.  

A mutation that destroys complementarity between regions 1 and 2 removes the 1–2 “pause” hairpin. Without this structure the timing of ribosome progression versus RNA polymerase elongation is altered; region 2 is more likely to remain free long enough to pair with region 3 even when tryptophan is abundant. Consequently the 2–3 antiterminator forms, the 3–4 terminator cannot form, and transcription continues through the operon.  

Options B and C favor or strengthen the terminator; D and E do not alter the 3–4 pairing decision. Thus only A directly prevents terminator formation under repressing conditions.}

